  "body": "Part of #308. Depends on #316 (setup transform).\n\n## Research outcome (public literature)\nRe-sampling a volumetric workpiece onto a new axis-aligned grid after a flip or 90 deg turn loses accuracy at the grid pitch, for single dexel and tri-dexel alike - tri-dexel reduces directional bias but is not rotation-invariant (Stifter 1995, dexel critique; Inui et al., triple-dexel; Museth 2013 / OpenVDB keep the sparse grid separate from its transform). The recommended design is one **canonical workpiece in the part frame**, evaluated through each setup's transform, re-sampled only where an axis-aligned grid is unavoidable. Exact B-rep booleans (#27 Solidcore) avoid resampling at a higher cost; the field/SDF engine and dual contouring (#23, PR #280) already exist.\n\n## Today\n`stock_sim::simulate_document` -> `clip_to_setup_stock` only lowers dexel columns to the next block's top - it never moves what the previous setup cut; a rotated or from-model stock in a later setup refuses the sim outright.\n\n## Proposal\n- Carry setup N's machined body into setup N+1 through the transform: the field engine in the part frame, sampled through the transform; the dexel engine re-samples on entry where it must, with the pitch stated in the verdict.\n- Where it cannot judge (no transform, unsupported stock kind) keep the conservative CLIP or the named refusal - never a guessed body.\n\n## Done means\nA two-setup part (face + pocket on top, flip, pocket underneath) simulates with the underside pocket cut into what setup 1 left; the verdict matches a single-setup reference within a stated tolerance; refusals stay named.\n",
